Create a class called `Employee` (instance fields: `empID`, `name`, `age`, `Salary`) with necessary constructor and methods corresponding to the Employee instances used in the `EmployeeClassDemo` as shown in the Fig 1:

```java
public class EmployeeClassDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Employee[] staff = {
            new Employee( 1, "Anil", 25, 50000 ),
            new Employee( 2, "John", 35, 60000 ),
            new Employee( 3, "Vinod", 38, 40000 )
        };

        for( int i = 0 ; i < staff.length; i++ )
            staff[i].raiseSalary( 5 ); // Raise everyone's salary by 5%

        for( int i = 0 ; i < staff.length; i++ )
            System.out.println( staff[i] );
    }
}
```

